<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/living-room.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/living-room-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/living-room.webp 1023w" sizes="100vw" width="1023" height="704" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li>Appraisal Q&amp;A</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Answers by topic</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Questions and Answers for Ohio</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers to the questions Ohio owners ask most — the appraisal process, costs and timing, policy rules, insurers and winter and storm claims.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-red" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-w" href="/contact-our-team/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><nav class="toc" aria-label="Topics"><p class="toc-h">Jump to a topic</p><ul><li><a href="#q-0">Appraisal basics</a></li><li><a href="#q-1">Cost and timing</a></li><li><a href="#q-2">Ohio policies and rules</a></li><li><a href="#q-3">Working with your insurer</a></li><li><a href="#q-4">Storm and winter claims</a></li></ul></nav><h2 id="q-0">Appraisal basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What does an insurance appraiser do in an Ohio claim?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>A party appraiser is chosen by you or your insurer under the policy’s appraisal clause to value the damage. Your appraiser inspects the property, builds or reviews a line-item estimate, and negotiates the amount of loss with the insurer’s appraiser, with an umpire breaking any deadlock.</p></div></details><details><summary>When does appraisal make sense?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal fits when the insurer agrees the loss is covered, at least in part, but you disagree on what it costs to repair or replace. It is a poor fit when the claim is denied outright or the dispute is about whether an exclusion applies.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is an appraisal award final?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>On the amount of loss, an award signed by two of the three panel members is generally binding. Courts can set awards aside in narrow circumstances, such as fraud or a panel deciding something outside its authority.</p></div></details><details><summary>How is appraisal different from hiring a public adjuster?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>A public adjuster represents you throughout the claim, including presenting it to the insurer. A party appraiser works within the appraisal clause to value a disputed loss. We are appraisers, not public adjusters or attorneys.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can my contractor be my appraiser?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many policies require an appraiser who is impartial or independent, and a contractor who expects to do the work usually has a stake in the result. Using one can give the insurer grounds to challenge the appraiser or the award.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-1">Cost and timing</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays for appraisal?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Under most policies each side pays its own appraiser and the two sides split the umpire’s fee. Ask any appraiser to describe their fee arrangement in writing before you sign.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long does an Ohio appraisal take?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many residential appraisals wrap up in a few weeks to a few months, depending on how fast appraisers are named and whether an umpire is needed. Large commercial or multi-building losses can take longer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it for a small gap?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the size of the gap compared with what appraisal will cost you. A gap of a few hundred dollars rarely justifies it, while a disputed roof, basement or whole-house scope often does. Our claim gap and fee estimator can help you compare.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does demanding appraisal stop the suit-limitation clock?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Do not assume it does. Many Ohio policies set a deadline to file suit, often one year from the date of loss, and whether appraisal affects it depends on the policy language. Check your policy and talk to an Ohio attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I need an umpire every time?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. If the two appraisers agree on the full amount, they sign the award themselves and the umpire never rules. An umpire is still usually chosen early so one is ready if needed.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-2">Ohio policies and rules</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does Ohio law require an appraisal clause?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal clauses come from the policy contract rather than a specific Ohio statute requiring them, but nearly every Ohio homeowners and commercial property form includes one. Read yours for the exact wording, time limits and appraiser standards.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who regulates insurers in Ohio?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The Ohio Department of Insurance in Columbus licenses insurers and enforces claim-handling rules, including the unfair claims settlement practices rule in Ohio Administrative Code 3901-1-54. You can file a consumer complaint with the department if you believe a claim is being mishandled.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I sue my insurer for bad faith in Ohio?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Ohio has no first-party bad-faith statute, but Ohio courts recognize a common-law bad-faith claim against an insurer that lacks reasonable justification for how it handles or pays a claim. Whether that applies to you is a legal question for an Ohio attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is the Ohio FAIR Plan?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The Ohio FAIR Plan Underwriting Association is the state’s property insurer of last resort for owners who cannot find coverage in the regular market. Its policies have their own terms, so read the loss-settlement and appraisal sections closely.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do Ohio policies use percentage deductibles?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Some do. Certain Ohio policies apply a wind or hail deductible of 1%–5% of the dwelling limit, which can be much larger than a flat deductible. Check your declarations page before you estimate what you will be paid.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-3">Working with your insurer</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Should I tell my adjuster before demanding appraisal?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It is usually smart to send a written supplement request first and give the insurer a chance to respond. If the gap remains, the demand letter will make clear that you tried to resolve it informally.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can the insurer refuse appraisal?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>An insurer may argue the dispute is about coverage rather than amount, or that a policy condition was not met. If that happens, get the objection in writing and consider talking to an Ohio attorney about your options.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will demanding appraisal hurt my relationship with the insurer?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal is a normal contract procedure, not a lawsuit, and insurers deal with it routinely. Keeping communication courteous and focused on price helps the process move.</p></div></details><details><summary>What happens to payments already made?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Prior payments are credited against the award. The insurer typically pays the award amount, less the deductible and anything already paid, under the policy’s loss-settlement terms.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-4">Storm and winter claims</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can appraisal be used for hail damage on an Ohio roof?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes, hail disputes are one of the most common uses of appraisal in Ohio. Typical disagreements include the number of damaged slopes, repair versus replacement, matching, and pricing for slate or specialty roofing.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is basement water damage eligible for appraisal?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>If the insurer accepts that the water is covered, for example under a sewer and drain backup endorsement, appraisal can resolve the amount. If the dispute is whether the water counts as flood or backup, that is a coverage question appraisal usually cannot answer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are ice dam leaks covered?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many Ohio homeowners policies cover interior water damage from an ice dam, while excluding the cost of fixing the underlying wear or maintenance. Wording varies, so check your policy and photograph the ice, the leak and the interior before cleanup.</p></div></details><details><summary>What should I document after a derecho or windstorm?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Photograph every elevation of the home, fallen trees, fences and outbuildings, and keep receipts for tarps, tree removal and emergency repairs. Save the date and time of the storm, since many policies measure deadlines from the date of loss.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does a frozen pipe claim qualify if the house was vacant?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many policies limit or exclude freeze damage when a home was vacant or unheated unless you maintained heat or drained the system. Read the freezing and vacancy provisions closely before assuming the loss is covered.</p></div></details></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-h">Talk it through first</h2><p>The first conversation is free. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-white" href="/contact-our-team/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Resources</h2><ul class="side-list"><li><a href="/appraisal-timeline/">Appraisal timeline<svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-q-and-a/" aria-current="page">Appraisal Q&amp;A<svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-lexicon/">Appraisal lexicon<svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-briefings/">Briefings<svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li></ul></div></aside></div><section class="cta-strip"><div class="wrap cta-in reveal"><div><h2>Didn’t see your question?</h2><p>Call or email — we’re glad to talk it through.</p></div><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-white" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-w" href="/contact-our-team/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
